  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+2y}{4}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(4,-2),(2,1),$ and $(-1,3).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,-2)}=\\frac{4+2(-2)}{4}=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=\\frac{2+2(1)}{4}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=\\frac{-1+2(3)}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/e1ca6081e8077bcbc54de6a71e10ef1d278bbbc6b99842f1eee0d3dfcb71ebda.png)",
